    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and the full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes locality derivation prefer the postcode, falling back to the city.\n\n**`RegionPostcodeTable`** (owns postcode-range knowledge) gained a `regionOf(String postcode)` method that returns the region whose inclusive range contains the postcode, or `Optional.empty()` when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range. This reuses the existing `REGION_RANGES` table rather than duplicating the ranges.\n\n**`Owner.getLocality()`** now composes the two lookups:\n```java\nreturn RegionPostcodeTable.regionOf(this.postcode)\n    .orElseGet(() -> CityRegionTable.regionOf(this.city));\n```\n\nEach table keeps its single responsibility (postcode\u2192region, city\u2192region), and `getLocality()` just expresses the precedence. Known cities still resolve to the same region (their pinned postcodes fall in the matching range), while a city that shares a name is now disambiguated by its postcode \u2014 e.g. an unknown city with a `3000` postcode yields `VIC`, as the new test asserts.",
